    "dos": "HB DOS 1.0",
    "volume": "HBWARP",
    "files": [
        {
            "name": "CANVAS LINES",
            "language": "basic",
            "source": "10 REM ============================================================\n20 REM HB-1: THE OTHER SURFACE, AS A BAR CHART\n30 REM The tube is a character display; the canvas beside it is a\n40 REM framebuffer with coordinates. The same program reaches both:\n50 REM POKE and PRINT go to the tube, LINE and CIRCLE address the\n60 REM canvas. SCREEN 13 is the fixed-coordinate screen -- 320 by\n70 REM 200 -- that the drawing statements are measured in.\n80 REM ============================================================\n90 SCREEN 13\n100 CLS\n110 DIM HEIGHT(8)\n120 FOR I = 1 TO 8\n130   LET HEIGHT(I) = 20 + (I MOD 4) * 30 + I * 6\n140 NEXT I\n150 REM --- the axes: one across, one down ------------------------\n160 LINE (30, 20) - (30, 170), 15\n170 LINE (30, 170) - (310, 170), 15\n180 REM --- eight bars, each one LINE with the BF fill ------------\n190 FOR I = 1 TO 8\n200   LET X = 40 + (I - 1) * 34\n210   LET TOP = 170 - HEIGHT(I)\n220   LINE (X, TOP) - (X + 26, 169), I, BF\n230   REM --- and a tick above each one, so the scale is readable --\n240   LINE (X + 13, TOP - 4) - (X + 13, TOP - 1), 15\n250 NEXT I\n260 REM --- a circle for a title block, so the shape is not all bars\n270 CIRCLE (70, 45), 26, 14\n280 CIRCLE (70, 45), 16, 7\n290 REM --- the tube says what the canvas is showing --------------\n300 LOCATE 20, 3\n310 COLOR 15, 0\n320 PRINT \"CANVAS 320x200: AXES, EIGHT BARS, TWO CIRCLES\";\n330 LOCATE 22, 3\n340 PRINT \"EVERY ONE OF THEM A LINE STATEMENT, ON A FRAMEBUFFER.\";\n350 END\n"
        },
        {
            "name": "COLOUR CHART",
            "language": "basic",
            "source": "10 REM ============================================================\n20 REM HB-1: SIXTEEN STEPS OF PHOSPHOR\n30 REM The tube is monochrome, so a colour number is a brightness:\n40 REM 0 is the background and 15 the brightest ink. The attribute\n50 REM byte at 36864 holds a pair of them -- ink in the high nibble,\n60 REM paper in the low one -- and each cell is written directly, so\n70 REM the picture is in memory before it is on the glass.\n80 REM ============================================================\n90 CLS\n100 REM --- the left ladder: sixteen cells, ink 0 to 15 on black ---\n110 FOR STEP = 0 TO 15\n120   POKE 32768 + (4 + STEP) * 80 + 8, 128\n130   POKE 36864 + (4 + STEP) * 80 + 8, STEP * 16\n140 NEXT STEP\n150 REM --- and the same sixteen the other way up: ink 0 on paper ---\n160 FOR STEP = 0 TO 15\n170   POKE 32768 + (4 + STEP) * 80 + 20, 128\n180   POKE 36864 + (4 + STEP) * 80 + 20, STEP\n190 NEXT STEP\n200 REM --- a strip of the sixteen side by side, along row 22 -----\n210 FOR STEP = 0 TO 15\n220   POKE 32768 + 18 * 80 + (8 + STEP * 2), 128\n230   POKE 36864 + 18 * 80 + (8 + STEP * 2), STEP * 16\n240 NEXT STEP\n250 REM --- what the reader is looking at, in the tube's own voice -\n260 FOR I = 1 TO 30\n270   POKE 32768 + 2 * 80 + (2 + I), 45\n280 NEXT I\n290 LOCATE 2, 4\n300 COLOR 15, 0\n310 PRINT \"PHOSPHOR: EACH CELL IS ONE BYTE OF BRIGHTNESS\";\n320 LOCATE 16, 4\n330 PRINT \"INK 0..15 ON BLACK, THEN PAPER 0..15 UNDER BLACK INK:\";\n340 LOCATE 24, 4\n350 PRINT \"THE SAME BYTE THE POKE WRITES IS THE PICTURE.\";\n360 END\n"
        },
        {
            "name": "FRAME CLOCK",
            "language": "basic",
            "source": "10 REM ============================================================\n20 REM HB-1: TWO CLOCKS, ONE READABLE AND ONE THAT INTERRUPTS\n30 REM Registers six and seven of the I/O page are a 16-bit frame\n40 REM counter at $A006/$A007 (40966/40967), sixty ticks a second.\n50 REM ON TIMER is the other clock: TIMER ON arms it, and its\n60 REM handler is called between statements while the program works.\n70 REM ============================================================\n80 CLS\n90 PRINT \"HB-1 CLOCK WORK\"\n100 PRINT \"---------------\"\n110 PRINT \"Frame counter, low byte then high byte:\"\n120 PEEK 40966\n130 PEEK 40967\n140 REM --- the interrupt clock -----------------------------------\n150 TICKS = 0\n160 ON TIMER(0.01) GOSUB 400\n170 TIMER ON\n180 FOR STEP = 1 TO 30000\n190   WORK = STEP * 2\n200 NEXT STEP\n210 TIMER OFF\n220 PRINT \"The handler ran \" + STR$(TICKS) + \" time(s) inside the loop.\"\n230 REM --- the count becomes a drawing ---------------------------\n240 SCREEN 1\n250 CIRCLE (320, 260), 110\n260 CIRCLE (320, 260), 8\n270 FOR TICK = 0 TO TICKS\n280   ANGLE = TICK * 20\n290   X = 320 + 100 * SIN(ANGLE)\n300   Y = 260 - 100 * COS(ANGLE)\n310   SETPENCOLOR 80 + TICK * 15, 200, 255 - TICK * 15\n320   LINE (320, 260) - (X, Y)\n330 NEXT TICK\n340 PRINT \"Each spoke is one interrupt; the hub is the dial.\"\n350 PRINT \"The frame counter above is the clock a program can read.\"\n360 END\n400 REM --- the interrupt handler -----------------------------------\n410 TICKS = TICKS + 1\n420 RETURN\n",
            "locked": false
        },
        {
            "name": "MEMORY MAP",
            "language": "basic",
            "source": "10 REM ============================================================\n20 REM HB-1: THE MAP, READ RATHER THAN BELIEVED\n30 REM The self-test says the machine has 32K, a screen and eleven\n40 REM ROMs. This asks the address space instead: four PEEKs, one\n50 REM from each region the map documents, and a line about what\n60 REM answered. A hole in the map answers zero, which is a thing\n70 REM the machine does on purpose.\n80 REM ============================================================\n90 CLS\n100 PRINT \"HB-1 MEMORY MAP, FROM THE OUTSIDE IN\"\n110 PRINT \"-----------------------------------\"\n120 PRINT \"\"\n130 REM --- free RAM below the screen -----------------------------\n140 PRINT \"00000  FREE RAM\";\n150 POKE 1000, 165\n160 LET RAMBYTE = PEEK(1000)\n170 PRINT \"     WROTE 165, READ \"; RAMBYTE;\n180 REM --- the screen cells ---------------------------------------\n190 PRINT \"\"\n200 PRINT \"32768  SCREEN CELLS\";\n210 POKE 32768, 65\n220 LET CELL = PEEK(32768)\n230 PRINT \"   'A' IS 65, READ \"; CELL;\n240 REM --- the colour attributes ----------------------------------\n250 PRINT \"\"\n260 PRINT \"36864  ATTRIBUTES\";\n270 POKE 36864, 240\n280 LET ATTR = PEEK(36864)\n290 PRINT \"     INK 15 ON PAPER 0 IS 240, READ \"; ATTR;\n300 REM --- the I/O page: the cursor is a register -----------------\n310 PRINT \"\"\n320 PRINT \"40960  I/O REGISTERS\";\n330 LOCATE 14, 3\n340 LET COLREG = PEEK(40960)\n350 PRINT \"  CURSOR COLUMN REGISTER READS \"; COLREG;\n360 REM --- ROM: readable, and it refuses to be written ------------\n370 PRINT \"\"\n380 PRINT \"61440  ROM SPACE\";\n390 LET ROMBYTE = PEEK(61440)\n400 PRINT \"    A ROM BYTE READS \"; ROMBYTE;\n410 REM --- a hole: an address no region claims -------------------\n420 PRINT \"\"\n430 PRINT \"20000  A HOLE IN THE MAP\";\n440 LET HOLE = PEEK(20000)\n450 PRINT \"  ANSWERS \"; HOLE;\n460 REM --- and the one thing memory refuses ----------------------\n470 PRINT \"\"\n480 PRINT \"WRITING TO ROM IS REFUSED, NOT IGNORED.\"\n490 POKE 61440, 1\n500 PRINT \"THE POKE ABOVE WAS TOLD SO, IN THE MACHINE'S VOICE.\"\n510 END\n"
        },
        {
            "name": "RAM PROBE",
            "language": "basic",
            "source": "10 REM ============================================================\n20 REM HB-1: RAM THAT PROVES ITSELF\n30 REM The machine claims 32K of free RAM below the screen at\n40 REM $8000. This writes a pattern into it, reads three cells\n50 REM back with PEEK, then draws a bar for every step of the\n60 REM pattern it laid down.\n70 REM ============================================================\n80 CLS\n90 PRINT \"RAM PROBE - 32K BELOW THE SCREEN\"\n100 PRINT \"--------------------------------\"\n110 REM --- lay a pattern across the first kilobyte ---------------\n120 FOR ADDRESS = 0 TO 1023 STEP 4\n130   POKE ADDRESS, 64 + ((ADDRESS / 4) MOD 128)\n140 NEXT ADDRESS\n150 REM --- ask the machine what it actually holds ---------------\n160 PRINT \"Three cells, written then read:\"\n170 PEEK 0\n180 PEEK 4\n190 PEEK 8\n200 REM --- the same bytes decide a drawing ----------------------\n210 SCREEN 1\n220 FOR BAR = 0 TO 7\n230   VALUE = 64 + (BAR * 17)\n240   LEFT = 60 + BAR * 80\n250   SETPENCOLOR 60 + BAR * 20, 200 - BAR * 15, 120 + BAR * 10\n260   LINE (LEFT, 520) - (LEFT + 56, 520 - VALUE)\n270   LINE (LEFT, 520 - VALUE) - (LEFT + 56, 520 - VALUE)\n280   PRINT \"Bar \" + STR$(BAR) + \" drew a byte of \" + STR$(VALUE)\n290 NEXT BAR\n300 PRINT \"Each bar is one step of the pattern, 64, 81, 98 ...\"\n310 PRINT \"The top of RAM and the top of the drawing are the same idea.\"\n",
            "locked": false
        },
        {
            "name": "SOUND BOARD",
            "language": "basic",
            "source": "10 REM ============================================================\n20 REM HB-1: A SOUND BOARD IN EIGHT NOTES\n30 REM SOUND takes a frequency in hertz and a length in ticks of\n40 REM 1/18.2 of a second, which is the machine's own unit. Each\n50 REM note also POKEs a block into the tube, so what is heard and\n60 REM what is seen are the same event.\n70 REM ============================================================\n80 CLS\n90 PRINT \"HB-1 SOUND BOARD\"\n100 PRINT \"----------------\"\n110 PRINT \"\"\n120 DIM NOTE(8)\n130 LET NOTE(1) = 262\n140 LET NOTE(2) = 294\n150 LET NOTE(3) = 330\n160 LET NOTE(4) = 349\n170 LET NOTE(5) = 392\n180 LET NOTE(6) = 440\n190 LET NOTE(7) = 494\n200 LET NOTE(8) = 523\n210 REM --- the scale, one block per note, left to right -----------\n220 FOR I = 1 TO 8\n230   LET FREQ = NOTE(I)\n240   SOUND FREQ, 2\n250   LET BAR = 4\n260   FOR R = 1 TO BAR\n270     POKE 32768 + (8 + R) * 80 + 10 + I * 3, 128\n280   NEXT R\n290   LOCATE 18, 10\n300   PRINT \"NOTE \"; I; \" = \"; FREQ; \" HZ\";\n310   LET BAR = BAR + 1\n320 NEXT I\n330 REM --- and a chord: three notes, one after another ------------\n340 PRINT \"\"\n350 PRINT \"A CHORD, ONE NOTE AT A TIME:\"\n360 SOUND 262, 3\n370 SOUND 330, 3\n380 SOUND 392, 4\n390 PRINT \"C, E, G - THE SAME THREE BLOCKS, TALLER.\";\n400 REM --- silence, said out loud ---------------------------------\n410 SOUND OFF\n420 COLOR 15, 0\n430 LOCATE 24, 10\n440 PRINT \"THE TICKS ARE THE TUBE'S, NOT THE SPEAKER'S.\";\n450 END\n"
        },
        {
            "name": "SPRITE LAB",
            "language": "basic",
            "source": "10 REM ============================================================\n20 REM HB-1: SPRITES OUT OF A FRAMEBUFFER\n30 REM A sprite on this machine is a rectangle of the canvas copied\n40 REM out with GET and pasted back with PUT. So the machine can\n50 REM make one out of nothing: draw it once with PSET, take it, and\n60 REM then have as many as the memory allows. Three of them fly\n70 REM across the screen and the tube counts the frames.\n80 REM ============================================================\n90 SCREEN 13\n100 CLS\n110 REM --- draw a shuttle once, in the corner of the screen -----\n120 FOR ROW = 0 TO 7\n130   PSET (ROW, ROW), 12\n140   PSET (15 - ROW, ROW), 12\n150   PSET (ROW, 8 - ROW), 12\n160   PSET (15 - ROW, 8 - ROW), 12\n170 NEXT ROW\n180 FOR ROW = 6 TO 9\n190   LINE (3, ROW) - (12, ROW), 12\n200 NEXT ROW\n210 PSET (7, 4), 15\n220 PSET (8, 4), 15\n230 REM --- take it, then wipe the template off the screen ------\n240 GET (0, 0) - (15, 15), SHUTTLE\n250 CLS\n260 REM --- three of them, at three heights and three speeds -----\n270 LET A = 0\n280 LET B = 60\n290 LET C = 120\n300 FOR FRAME = 1 TO 40\n310   PUT (A, 40), SHUTTLE\n320   PUT (B, 110), SHUTTLE\n330   PUT (C, 180), SHUTTLE\n340   FOR WAIT = 1 TO 300\n350   NEXT WAIT\n360   LET A = (A + 7) MOD 320\n370   LET B = (B + 5) MOD 320\n380   LET C = (C + 3) MOD 320\n390   LOCATE 2, 3\n400   COLOR 15, 0\n410   PRINT \"FRAME \"; FRAME; \" OF 40\";\n420 NEXT FRAME\n430 REM --- the canvas keeps the last frame, so say what made it -\n440 LOCATE 4, 3\n450 PRINT \"ONE 16x16 SPRITE, TAKEN WITH GET, PUT 120 TIMES.\";\n460 END\n"
        },
        {
            "name": "TUBE WRITER",
            "language": "basic",
            "source": "10 REM ============================================================\n20 REM HB-1: THE TUBE IS MEMORY\n30 REM Every character below is written into the screen cells at\n40 REM $8000 (32768), so the tube shows it and PEEK reads it back.\n50 REM Each cell also has a colour byte at $9000 (36864):\n60 REM the ink in the high nibble, the paper in the low one.\n70 REM Run this on the HB-1 to paint its tube; the same program\n80 REM runs in the IDE, where the turtle part still draws.\n90 REM ============================================================\n100 CLS\n110 MESSAGE$ = \"HB-1  SCREEN MEMORY\"\n120 ROW = 2\n130 COL = 4\n140 REM --- one POKE per character, at an address the program computes\n150 FOR I = 1 TO LEN(MESSAGE$)\n160   CODE = ASC(MID$(MESSAGE$, I, 1))\n170   POKE 32768 + ROW * 80 + COL + I - 1, CODE\n180 NEXT I\n190 REM --- the same cells, colourised: ink 10 on paper 0 ----------\n200 FOR I = 0 TO LEN(MESSAGE$) - 1\n210   POKE 36864 + ROW * 80 + COL + I, 160\n220 NEXT I\n230 REM --- an underline drawn a row below, in the screen itself -----\n240 FOR I = 0 TO LEN(MESSAGE$) - 1\n250   POKE 32768 + (ROW + 1) * 80 + COL + I, 45\n260 NEXT I\n270 REM --- read the first cell back: the tube and PEEK agree -------\n280 PEEK 32768 + ROW * 80 + COL\n290 REM --- the cursor is an I/O register, so LOCATE is a write too\n300 LOCATE 5, 4\n310 PRINT \"Written by POKE, read by PEEK.\"\n320 REM --- border colour is register two of the I/O page ----------\n330 POKE 40962, 9\n340 REM --- and the canvas takes a turtle frame round the message --\n350 SCREEN 1\n360 SETPENCOLOR 90 190 255\n370 SETPENWIDTH 2\n380 FOR SIDE = 1 TO 4\n390   FORWARD 130\n400   RIGHT 90\n410 NEXT SIDE\n420 PRINT \"Tube and canvas both agree.\"\n",
            "locked": false
        },
        {
            "name": "TURTLE GARDEN",
            "language": "logo",
            "source": "10 REM ============================================================\n20 REM HB-1 IN LOGO: A HOUSE WITH A GARDEN\n30 REM The same machine, a different ROM. Nothing here is BASIC:\n40 REM in, or CS, or SETPC. Select LOGO in the ROM socket, load\n50 REM this listing, and the turtle draws on the graphics pane\n60 REM beside the tube -- the canvas the machine and the IDE share.\n70 REM ============================================================\n80 CS\n90 SETPC 6\n100 SETPENWIDTH 2\n110 REPEAT 4 [ FD 90 RT 90 ]\n120 REM --- the roof, a triangle over the walls ------------------\n130 PU\n140 HOME\n150 FD 90\n160 RT 90\n170 FD 90\n180 LT 90\n190 PD\n200 SETPC 4\n210 REPEAT 3 [ FD 90 RT 120 ]\n220 REM --- a door, in the middle of the front wall --------------\n230 PU\n240 HOME\n250 FD 90\n260 RT 90\n270 FD 40\n280 LT 90\n290 PD\n300 SETPC 1\n310 REPEAT 2 [ FD 40 RT 90 FD 25 RT 90 ]\n320 REM --- a sun, and a garden of flowers -----------------------\n330 PU\n340 HOME\n350 RT 90\n360 FD 200\n370 LT 90\n380 PD\n390 SETPC 6\n400 REPEAT 12 [ FD 30 BK 30 RT 30 ]\n410 PU\n420 HOME\n430 FD 130\n440 LT 90\n450 FD 60\n460 PD\n470 SETPC 2\n480 REPEAT 5 [ FD 20 RT 90 FD 40 RT 90 FD 20 LT 180 ]\n490 PU\n500 HOME\n510 FD 130\n520 LT 90\n530 FD 100\n540 PD\n550 REPEAT 5 [ FD 20 RT 90 FD 40 RT 90 FD 20 LT 180 ]\n560 PU\n570 HOME\n580 SETPC 7\n590 REPEAT 8 [ FD 90 RT 45 ]\n600 PRINT \"THE TURTLE IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE MACHINE.\"\n"
        }
    ]
}
